#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.13pt, 790.176pt, "12 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 82.08pt, "At the first layer boundary, only a very small difference wave is generated in the backward ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 97.34pt, "direction (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(121.94pt, 97.34pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(139.22pt, 97.34pt, " 6.53 µs). The difference wave in the forward direction is also initially rather ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 112.46pt, "small (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(105.02pt, 112.46pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(122.3pt, 112.46pt, " 5.35 µs), but then becomes increasingly larger as it propagates through the layer ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 127.7pt, "(")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(74.904pt, 127.7pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(92.184pt, 127.7pt, " 6.53 and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(139.94pt, 127.7pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(157.22pt, 127.7pt, " 7.52 µs). This is due to the ever-increasing time delay between the two var-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 142.46pt, "iation waves with increasing distance traveled through the layer. Only at the right boundary of ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 157.46pt, "the layer does this amplification process cease, and the difference wave continues to propagate ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 172.58pt, "with a constant amplitude in the matrix medium (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.21pt, 172.58pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(325.49pt, 172.58pt, " 8.91 and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(372.79pt, 172.58pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(390.07pt, 172.58pt, " 12.02 µs). ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 195.5pt, "From the right layer boundary, a strong difference wave is emitted in the forward direction and ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 210.74pt, "a smaller but significant difference wave in the reverse direction (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(379.51pt, 210.74pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(396.79pt, 210.74pt, " 8.91 µs). These two waves ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 225.86pt, "also dominate the final difference wave field at ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(299.69pt, 225.86pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(316.97pt, 225.86pt, " 12.08 µs. The waveforms of the difference ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 240.74pt, "waves again resemble the spatial derivative of the excitation signal, analogous to the case of ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 255.62pt, "geometric changes in Figure 5. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 301.49pt, "3.4 Change in the Density of the Layer at Constant Speed of Sound ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 324.29pt, "Analogous to the procedure shown in Figure 7, the density of the layer is varied by 1 kg/m³ up ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 339.17pt, "and down while keeping the speed of sound constant, resulting in maximum changes of approx-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 354.17pt, "imately 4 per mille (right column of Figure 8). ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 376.97pt, "During the density change, difference waves are generated at both layer boundaries. At the left ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 391.85pt, "layer boundary, two equally sized and in-phase difference waves are generated in the forward ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 407.21pt, "and reverse directions (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(182.3pt, 407.21pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(199.61pt, 407.21pt, " 6.53 µs). At the right layer boundary, however, a large out-of-phase ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 422.33pt, "reverse wave and a smaller forward wave remain (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(322.61pt, 422.33pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(339.91pt, 422.33pt, " 8.91 µs). Ultimately, the two out-of-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 437.21pt, "phase difference waves reflected at the layer boundaries exhibit the largest amplitudes, followed ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 452.45pt, "by the transmitted wave and a first multiple reflection from the layer at ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(438.91pt, 452.45pt, "𝑥=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(457.99pt, 452.45pt, " 30 mm (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(507.22pt, 452.45pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 467.23pt, "12.08 µs). In contrast to the geometric layer changes from Sections 3.1 and 3.2, the difference ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 482.11pt, "waves here have the same waveform as the initial wave field. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 527.95pt, "3.5 Interim Conclusion ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 550.87pt, "The investigations in this section have shown that the difference wave fields resulting from the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 565.75pt, "changes to the initial model fulfill all requirements 1-7 from Section 2 for the information meas-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 580.63pt, "ure to be determined. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 603.43pt, "Each structural parameter of the system can be considered separately (requirement 1) and each ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 618.43pt, "leads to entirely individual and clearly distinguishable difference wave fields, all of which result ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 633.31pt, "from one and the same initial wave field (see the last partial images of the difference wave ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 648.55pt, "fields in Figure 5 and Figure")
  #line-at(213.17pt, 648.55pt, "8 for ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(240.17pt, 648.55pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(257.45pt, 648.55pt, " 12.08 µs). Difference wave fields are therefore clearly ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 663.34pt, "much better suited for classification tasks than absolute wave fields, which differ only mini-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 678.22pt, "mally from the initial wave field with each variation, a difference that is practically undetectable ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 693.1pt, "to the naked eye (and also to AI systems). ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 716.02pt, "Since the considered difference calculation of the variational models refers to the total wave ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 730.9pt, "field, which is composed of the incident and scattered fields, the two identical excitation fields ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 745.776pt, "are cancelled out in the difference calculation. This corresponds to requirements 2 and 3 from ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 760.776pt, "Section 2, namely that the excitation field carries no information before its interaction with the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 775.656pt, "scatterer and that the source of the excitation wave (usually the transducer) cannot, in general, ")
