  #line-at(70.944pt, 180.86pt, "In the following section, we will use the simulation examples from Section 3 to check whether ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 195.74pt, "the quantities (75) - (83) derived here actually represent a physically meaningful and consistent ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 210.62pt, "description of the generation and propagation of information in the ultrasonic wave field. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(88.944pt, 256.46pt, "5.")
  #line-at(106.94pt, 256.46pt, "Verification of the Derived Information Quantities ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 279.41pt, "We revisit simulation examples 3.1–3.4 from Figure 5 and Figure 8, evaluating the information ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 294.29pt, "parameters determined in Section 4 for each case. For comparison purposes, we start with the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 309.17pt, "energy balance in the initial model. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 354.89pt, "5.1 Energy Balance in the Initial Model")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 377.81pt, "Figure 10 initially shows the analogous quantities from the balance equation (10)  for the kinetic ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 392.69pt, "energy density in the initial model as time snapshots, in order to better assess similarities and ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 407.57pt, "differences with the subsequent information quantities. The individual energy quantities are ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 422.45pt, "each normalized to the maximum of the initial signal arriving from the left. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 445.37pt, "The kinetic energy density in the left column of Figure 10 shows only positive values due to ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 460.25pt, "the squaring of the particle velocity. Its maximum amplitude visibly decreases within the layer ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 475.51pt, "(")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(74.904pt, 475.51pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(92.184pt, 475.51pt, " 6.53 µs) and then increases again after passing through the layer (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(414.91pt, 475.51pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(432.19pt, 475.51pt, " 8.91 µs). It should ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 490.39pt, "be noted that the signal waveform also broadens within the layer due to the longer wavelength ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 505.27pt, "present there. Therefore, the decrease in the maximum amplitude when passing through the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 520.15pt, "layer does not mean that the total energy density in the signal decreases. As we will see below, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 535.03pt, "the integral of the energy density over the total signal remains unchanged even within the layer! ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 557.95pt, "Slight fluctuations in the waveform are visible during the interaction with the layer boundaries ")
