#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.13pt, 790.176pt, "33 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 82.08pt, "Unlike energy density, the Poynting vector can take on both positive and negative values. In ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 96.98pt, "the one-dimensional case presented here, positive values indicate propagation from left to right ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 111.86pt, "(forward direction), while negative values indicate propagation in the opposite direction. In the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 127.1pt, "images for ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(125.3pt, 127.1pt, "𝑡≥")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(142.7pt, 127.1pt, "  6.53 µs, the leftward-propagating reflections are just barely visible with small ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 141.98pt, "negative values in the left part of the model. The reflection from the left layer boundary exhibits ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 157.22pt, "a negative amplitude before interacting with the left model boundary (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(415.39pt, 157.22pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(432.67pt, 157.22pt, " 8.91 µs), which is ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 172.34pt, "converted into a positive value (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(224.33pt, 172.34pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(241.61pt, 172.34pt, " 12.08 µs) by the change in direction during the subsequent ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 187.22pt, "reflection. This is indicated in the two corresponding images by small vertical arrows and cor-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 202.1pt, "responding insets. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 225.02pt, "The source term of the kinetic energy is shown in the right-hand column of Figure 10. It has a ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 239.9pt, "significantly different shape than the other two energy quantities, which is due to the spatial ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 254.78pt, "derivative of the particle velocity. The source term assumes both positive and negative values, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 269.66pt, "reflecting the continuous conversion of kinetic energy to potential energy (negative source ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 284.57pt, "term) and from potential to kinetic energy (positive source term). It decreases slightly within ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 299.45pt, "the layer and also results in only small, barely visible reflections at both layer boundaries. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 322.37pt, "If the total energy density were displayed instead of the kinetic energy density in the left column ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 337.25pt, "of Figure 10, the source term in the right column would be omitted. It would then be zero at all ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 352.49pt, "times except for the excitation point at ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(256.97pt, 352.49pt, "𝑥= 0")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(286.01pt, 352.49pt, ", where the ultrasonic energy input by the probe is ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 367.37pt, "implemented in the model. The snapshots for the total energy density according to Eqs. (12) ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 382.25pt, "and (14), which are not shown here, have essentially the same form as those of the kinetic ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 397.13pt, "energy density; however, there are slight deviations at the moment of interaction with the two ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 412.01pt, "layer boundaries, and the normalization factor is twice as large. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 434.93pt, "In addition to the image sequences from Figure 10, the energy content in the model under con-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 449.81pt, "sideration can also be represented as a function of time. For this purpose, the kinetic energy, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 464.69pt, "the potential energy according to Eq. (14), and the sum of both are added over all grid cells of ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 479.95pt, "the numerical model and displayed as a function of time for the period of interest up to ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(507.22pt, 479.95pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 494.71pt, "12.08 µs (Figure 11). ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 517.63pt, "It can be seen that the energy content increases sharply during the duration of the excitation ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 532.51pt, "signal (1 µs) and then remains essentially constant. In the case of kinetic and potential energy, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 547.39pt, "temporary deviations from this plateau are observable, occurring at the time of the primary ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 562.63pt, "interaction of the incident wave with the left layer boundary (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(370.51pt, 562.63pt, "𝑡≈")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(387.67pt, 562.63pt, " 5.5 µs), with the right layer ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 577.87pt, "boundary (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(124.34pt, 577.87pt, "𝑡≈")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(141.5pt, 577.87pt, " 7.7 µs), and again with the interaction of the wave originally reflected at the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 592.99pt, "right layer boundary with the left layer boundary (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(311.45pt, 592.99pt, "𝑡≈")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(328.61pt, 592.99pt, " 10.5 µs). During these interactions, self-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 607.87pt, "interference occurs between parts of the incident wave and parts of the already reflected wave, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 622.75pt, "causing temporary deviations from the constant plateau. However, a comparison of the kinetic ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 637.63pt, "and potential energy curves shows that these deviations occur exactly opposite in phase in both ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 652.51pt, "cases, so that the sum of the two vanishes and the remaining plateau demonstrates the energy ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 667.42pt, "conservation according to Eq. (13) after removal of the external forces (last image in Figure 11). ")
]

