#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.13pt, 790.176pt, "49 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 82.08pt, "In contrast to all previous representations, in which we depicted information on a normalized ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 96.98pt, "scale, Figure 24 makes use of one of the greatest advantages of the information theory presented ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 111.86pt, "here: the existence of an absolute information scale. This allows for a direct quantitative com-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 126.74pt, "parison between physically different parameters and different measurement setups, such as re-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 141.62pt, "flection and transmission. As we explained above, information is a physically dimensionless ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 156.5pt, "quantity. Nevertheless, it makes sense to introduce a base unit for information in analogy to the ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 171.38pt, "bits")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(88.344pt, 171.38pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(112.1pt, 171.38pt, "bytes")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(136.7pt, 171.38pt, " of Shannon's information theory. We refer to this fundamental unit of structural ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 186.26pt, "information as the 'Change Bit', or ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(239.09pt, 186.26pt, "Cbit")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(259.85pt, 186.26pt, " for short, because it represents a measure of the (abso-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 201.26pt, "lute) change of the wave field for a given (relative) parameter change. Other suitable terms ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 216.14pt, "would be 'Structural Bit' or 'Parameter Bit'. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 238.94pt, "All information densities in Figure 24 are given in Cbits/m in the present 1D model (usually ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 253.94pt, "Cbits/m³ in 3-D), allowing for quantitative comparisons. The decisive factor is not so much the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 268.82pt, "maximum of the signals, but rather the integral over all information contributions occurring ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 283.97pt, "within the time window of interest, here up to ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(293.69pt, 283.97pt, "𝑡=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(310.97pt, 283.97pt, " 20 µs. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 306.89pt, "For Example 3.1 (layer position at constant thickness), 100% of the information occurs on the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 321.77pt, "reflection side, since no information about the layer position is encoded in the transmitted sig-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 336.65pt, "nal. A maximum peak of 4184.2 Cbits/m is recorded in the reflected signal; the integral over ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 351.53pt, "both reflections comprises 102.41 Cbits/m, based on the 20 µs time window. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 374.45pt, "For Example 3.2 (layer thickness at constant position), the maximum amplitude is indeed on ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 389.33pt, "the transmission side (208.94 Cbits/m compared to 167.37 Cbits/m in reflection), but the inte-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 404.21pt, "gral of the information across both reflections comprises a total of 3.2991 Cbits/m compared to ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 419.09pt, "only 2.6356 Cbits/m on the transmission side. Thus, 55.6% of the information is provided in ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 433.97pt, "the reflected signal and 44.4% in the transmitted signal. Overall, the integrated value from both ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 448.85pt, "signals is 5.9347 Cbits/m, which is less than 6% of the total information from Example 3.1. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 463.85pt, "Therefore, with the same relative parameter change, the initial model examined here generates ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 478.75pt, "approximately 17 times more information in the case of a change in position than in the case of ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 493.63pt, "a change in thickness. This difference becomes clear when we look again at the corresponding ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 508.51pt, "difference wave fields in Figure 5. For example, the maximum amplitudes of the first layer echo ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 523.39pt, "were practically identical in both examples (approximately 1.6% of the initial signal). In Ex-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 538.63pt, "ample 3.1, however, this was caused by a relative parameter change of ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(416.35pt, 538.63pt, "2∆𝑥25")
  #line-at(436.87pt, 538.15pt, "⁄")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(456.79pt, 538.63pt, " mm, while in ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 553.87pt, "Example 3.2, the relative change was five times larger, at ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(362.11pt, 553.87pt, "4∆𝑥10")
  #line-at(382.63pt, 553.39pt, "⁄")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(402.55pt, 553.87pt, " mm and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(451.39pt, 553.87pt, "10∆𝑥25")
  #line-at(478.66pt, 553.39pt, "⁄")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(498.58pt, 553.87pt, " mm, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 568.63pt, "respectively. Squaring the factor of 5 yields a value 25 times larger in terms of information, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 583.51pt, "which corresponds almost exactly to the observed ratio of the maximum amplitudes of ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 598.39pt, "4184.2 Cbits/m (Example 3.1) to 167.37 Cbits/m (Example 3.2) for the first reflection. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 621.31pt, "In Example 3.3 (speed of sound in the layer at constant density), 96.2% of the information is ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 636.19pt, "provided in the transmitted signal and only 3.8% in the reflected signal. This results in inte-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 651.07pt, "grated information values of 166.48 Cbits/m in transmission and 6.5552 Cbits/m in reflection, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 665.98pt, "cumulatively 173.04 Cbits/m. The maximum peaks are 13491.0 Cbits/m for the transmitted ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 680.86pt, "signal and 550.85 Cbits/m for the reflected signal. Example 3.3 thus generates the most infor-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 695.74pt, "mation of all four examples, which is primarily due to the fact that the source present here is ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 710.74pt, "effective not only at the layer boundaries but also throughout its volume. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 733.54pt, "For the last example 3.4 (density of the layer at constant speed of sound), the lowest information ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 748.416pt, "values of all the examples are obtained. The integrated values are 0.0953 Cbits/m (96.3%) for ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 763.416pt, "the reflection side and 0.0037 Cbits/m (3.7%) for the transmission side, cumulatively 0.099 ")
]

