  #line-at(106.94pt, 416.81pt, "Relationship with Shannon and Fisher Information ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 439.73pt, "The unique aspect of the “change information” introduced in this paper lies in the fact that, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 454.61pt, "unlike all other known information measures such as Shannon or Fisher information, it does ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 469.51pt, "not require a probabilistic or statistical basis, but can instead be motivated and described purely ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 484.39pt, "deterministically. All simulations used are based on physical-deterministic fundamental equa-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 499.27pt, "tions and yield the same result with each repetition (apart from minimal and insignificant nu-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 514.15pt, "merical deviations). Neither expected values and variances nor other ensemble-averaged ther-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 529.03pt, "modynamic quantities need to be used. This suggests that the change information represents ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 543.91pt, "fundamental physical theory, which only takes on a statistical character in conjunction with ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 558.91pt, "real, noisy measurement data. In the following, we briefly discuss the mathematically motivated ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 573.79pt, "Shannon information and the statistical Fisher information in order to explore the differences ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 588.67pt, "and potential connecting factors with the new change information. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 634.39pt, "8.1 Shannon Information: Information as a Surprise ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 657.34pt, "The mathematical information theory established by Claude Shannon (see, e.g., [1, 4]) deals ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 672.22pt, "with concepts such as information and entropy, transmission of information, as well as data ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 687.1pt, "compression and coding. It is of paramount importance, particularly for telecommunications, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 701.98pt, "but also for all types of communication systems. Shannon's theory uses the thermodynamic ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 716.86pt, "concept of entropy to characterize the information content of messages transmitted over a noisy ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 731.74pt, "communication channel. For example, if the message to be transmitted is given by a set of ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 747.096pt, "characters ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(122.42pt, 747.096pt, "𝑋= {𝑥")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
