  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 82.08pt, "excitation signal, it is ensured that the final information quantities in the linear-elastic model ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 96.98pt, "under consideration do not depend on the excitation amplitude and that the information itself ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 111.86pt, "becomes physically dimensionless. Nevertheless, the derived quantities provide an absolute in-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 126.74pt, "formation scale, the base units of which are called 'Change Bits', or ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(394.15pt, 126.74pt, "Cbits")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(419.59pt, 126.74pt, " for short. This allows ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 141.62pt, "for the quantitative comparison of different system parameters as well as different measurement ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 156.5pt, "setups. As it turns out, both changes in sound velocity and changes in position generate a par-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 171.38pt, "ticularly large amount of information. While reflection setups are particularly well-suited from ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 186.26pt, "an information-theoretical perspective for density and position changes, transmission setups are ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 201.26pt, "the preferred method for sound velocity changes. Thickness changes can be readily detected in ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 216.14pt, "both transmission and reflection. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 238.94pt, "The change information presented in this work has no connection with the information-tech-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 253.94pt, "nical Shannon information, but it does show interesting analogies to statistical Fisher infor-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 268.82pt, "mation. Unlike Fisher information, however, the change information has a purely deterministic ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 283.73pt, "basis, suggesting that it represents the more fundamental theory from a physical perspective. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 298.61pt, "Applying this theory to real, noisy measurement signals will likely lead to forms of Fisher in-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 313.49pt, "formation, but it cannot be equated with it, as it is claimed for the electromagnetic field in [8]. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 328.37pt, "The possible relationship between these two information theories should be the subject of future ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 343.25pt, "investigations. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 389.09pt, "9.2 Outlook ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 411.89pt, "The information theory presented in this paper was derived using the simplified case of plane ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 426.77pt, "longitudinal waves and is therefore only valid in idealized one-dimensional systems. An exten-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 441.77pt, "sion to more realistic 2-D and 3-D systems is the subject of ongoing work. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 464.57pt, "As already described, certain finite system variations can also be implemented experimentally. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 479.47pt, "Thus, the generation, annihilation, and flow of information can also be visualized and investi-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 494.47pt, "gated experimentally. This, too, is the subject of ongoing work. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 517.27pt, "The examples shown referred to a single initial model, i.e., a specific model configuration in ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 532.15pt, "parameter space. However, each point in this parameter space generally exhibits different de-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 547.39pt, "rivatives of the field quantities ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(218.57pt, 547.39pt, "𝑣")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(225.41pt, 547.39pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(247.97pt, 547.39pt, "𝑇")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(255.41pt, 547.39pt, " with respect to the parameters under consideration. This ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 562.27pt, "results in multidimensional information landscapes and thus information-theoretic weighting ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 577.15pt, "factors in the parameter space, which can serve as a starting point for targeted search and cov-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 592.03pt, "erage strategies for the most efficient generation of new experimental or simulated training data ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 606.91pt, "with high quality. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 629.83pt, "Further interesting applications were already discussed in Section 7. These include, in particu-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 644.71pt, "lar, extending the theory to several simultaneously changing parameters, developing new eval-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 659.62pt, "uation methods and imaging techniques based on difference wave fields, and developing new ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 674.5pt, "information-based machine learning methods. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 697.42pt, "The concept of change information presented in this work is the first information theory to be ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 712.3pt, "based on a deterministic, i.e., non-statistical, foundation and therefore represents a fundamental ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 727.18pt, "theory from a physical perspective. Its scope is by no means limited to ultrasonic testing and ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 742.056pt, "can be readily applied to all situations in NDE and physics in which physical fields (e.g., acous-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 756.936pt, "tic, electromagnetic, thermal, gravitational, etc.) interact with objects. The specific balance ")
]

