  #line-at(354.31pt, 291.53pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(501.94pt, 304.37pt, "(92) ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 339.77pt, "This also provides a plausible basis for an information measure, as the following simple exam-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 354.77pt, "ple shows. If, for instance, the number of acoustic emission events per minute in the system ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 370.01pt, "increases by ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(134.3pt, 370.01pt, "∆𝑀=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(164.3pt, 370.01pt, " 2 events per minute due to a parameter change, then stating this absolute ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 385.13pt, "value is not particularly meaningful as long as it is unclear whether, e.g., ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(428.95pt, 385.13pt, "𝑀")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(438.43pt, 387.53pt, "0")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(447.19pt, 385.13pt, "=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(456.19pt, 385.13pt, " 10 events per ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 400.37pt, "minute or ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(119.54pt, 400.37pt, "𝑀")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(129.02pt, 402.77pt, "0")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(137.78pt, 400.37pt, "=")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(146.78pt, 400.37pt, "1000 events per minute were measured before the parameter change. Only after ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 415.49pt, "dividing by ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(130.22pt, 415.49pt, "𝑀")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(139.7pt, 417.89pt, "0")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(145.1pt, 415.49pt, ", informative statements emerge, such as a 20 percent change or a 0.2 percent ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 430.37pt, "change. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 453.17pt, "Since the information concept presented here is based on changes in measured field quantities, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 468.19pt, "and a change can in principle be defined as either an absolute or a relative quantity, it is not ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 483.07pt, "surprising that the derived information measure also exists in both absolute and relative formu-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 497.95pt, "lations. While the relative formulation appears to be the only possible one for passive measure-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 512.83pt, "ments, both formulations are conceivable for active measurements. In this work, the absolute ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 527.71pt, "formalism with normalization to the energy of the excitation signal was preferred, but a relative ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 542.59pt, "formulation would also be possible in principle. The differences between the two formulations ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 557.47pt, "are best illustrated by a simple example. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 580.39pt, "For instance, if we obtain two echoes of different strengths with amplitudes of 1 and 1/2, re-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 595.27pt, "spectively, as a result of an active pulse-echo measurement on the initial model, and then per-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 610.15pt, "form a parameter change that alters both echoes by the same factor (i.e., halves them), the ab-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 625.03pt, "solute formulation of the information presented in this work according to Eq. (91) yields four ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 639.91pt, "times the information content for the first echo as for the second. From a measurement perspec-")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 654.82pt, "tive, this makes perfect sense because large absolute changes are generally easier to measure ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 669.7pt, "than small ones, especially when the latter are almost hidden in noise. In contrast, the relative ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 685.3pt, "formulation of the information with normalization to ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(323.09pt, 685.3pt, "𝑀")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(332.59pt, 687.94pt, "0")
  #line-at(333.55pt, 680.74pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(339.07pt, 685.3pt, " according to Eq. (92) leads to the same ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 700.06pt, "information content for both echoes. This can also be useful if both echoes are well above the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 715.06pt, "noise threshold. The specific choice of absolute or relative formulation of information therefore ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 729.94pt, "depends on the respective application, the applicable framework conditions, and the research ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 744.816pt, "questions being pursued. The advantages and disadvantages of both approaches can only be ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 759.696pt, "properly assessed once experience with further applications is available in the future. ")
]

